#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166637 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30b55397-ef25-5e49-bee0-eb619e97b6ad" :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "c69f45e6-f7e8-5339-9de9-0168b5a11e31" :question/math-academy-id "e-8437" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c913ab811a695efe6151a72e2b9ace0e1eabd853e41d272a4e3720457b9f54ac.png)\nConsider the matrix shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the matrices $U$ and $V.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that since $A^T \\! A$ is diagonal, the singular values of $A$ are \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\sigma_1 = \\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$: Seeking non-zero solutions of $(A^T\\!A-2I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0},$ we obtain the unit eigenvectors \n\n$\\qquad$ ![](images/a4/a41a31eedadef820e4ba39ff9219723b663967a70546d2b845f17a527ad5d2c7.png) and ![](images/40/403e2da8a3f4686091140b901d53679de7adb76210df2ba836288e1ebe15683f.png)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/7a/7a2d2aec43b7653bae77bdf4f315281d1d44865c9a6cb1b0c52db09f5de9c79a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166638 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99270a04-7542-5545-92a3-cf13464ef8bc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Columns of the First Matrix Given Part of an SVD With a Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "0324936b-a3b5-50ae-b064-dfff93c4dafb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8438" :question/problem "![](images/cc/cc3969660197510f4c7abb016b6d4ed593b8eb69e59fc9b1396c4c1ddcc1e549.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and $\\sigma_i \\neq 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\sigma_1=2\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sigma_2=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & -1 \\\\ -\\sqrt{3} & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is $\\sigma_2=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n![](images/e0/e0566311c99934171da98a96e1bed902588698ac948f817cea20d98920e64581.png)\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\|\\mathbf{u}_2\\|=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} x_1 + \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} x_2 = 0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution ![](images/e6/e6415e2bc0b61c20486c61216ca322e3bdc0e6efb9ae2570c2b255b898225432.png) Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/f0/f053fd32c444f585085929ac6908e1d2bc66fb4dad71bdf64c6498a026f9d237.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166639 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0dc37d-a22e-572c-933d-f3490ad356de" :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix With a Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "4775354e-9a25-5e24-a1ef-57265f724a8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8439" :question/problem "![](images/a3/a327c5e958fefa27506572babfa7673092a5f73024a91fa23ac12a99a2b8092a.png)\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $\\Sigma$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the matrices $U$ and $V.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}32 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that $\\sigma_1=2\\sqrt{10}$ and $\\sigma_2=0.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$):\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=\\sigma_1^2=40,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-40I & =[\\begin{aligned}32 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 8\\end{aligned}]-40[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -16 \\\\ -16 & -32\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 40I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives a unit eigenvector\n![](images/1c/1c07efd18aff72d18dc8725832e33160c001e0d441b0ef0748a519389ea1bff4.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=\\sigma_2^2=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}32 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 8\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}32 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A^T \\! A - 0I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives a unit eigenvector\n![](images/14/14bda31a91349b26190594065760f44f14e329f648b7e5f8a6d84c03d803ee4b.png)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since the first singular value is not zero, and we know the corresponding singular right vector, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{10}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is $\\sigma_2=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n![](images/1b/1b0599cec4f025662f6f98b17952bd0e039d59a93fa2a7914067a74e4622f793.png)\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\|\\mathbf{u}_2\\|=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} x_1 + \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} x_2 = 0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution ![](images/34/34e66b511d6b341028b98d3676de1672feef643210fc0fe146969e0b3f73b542.png) Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/c1/c1bda4ad7d7b0e8457622db5bb40fb6744819c457bb6c9928c9ca7fe2f5d963b.png)\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/54/54448d8e2782a9be652cbc492c5d0f570de360a82f2913e8bc81bdec6c333cc1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30b55397-ef25-5e49-bee0-eb619e97b6ad" :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf6d710e-9d76-58a7-9875-783b94d05dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369813} {:answer/id #uuid "50a8ba0b-f51d-51ca-a5be-2de475bcd33b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369814} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b3d609-3f4a-5b0d-8a7b-195a36d6ddeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369815} {:answer/id #uuid "48e9e079-bebe-5381-860b-a8ac54ca389e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and  \n$V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369816} {:answer/id #uuid "d41306dc-2983-50ef-bb64-3ff0bf1f7038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50a8ba0b-f51d-51ca-a5be-2de475bcd33b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369814} :answer-field/id #uuid "79542f16-b7e8-51bf-8fd6-8fdd04d2b6ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "449ce30b-f10c-5a2b-ab82-067987cf9dce" :question/math-academy-id "q-140959" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that since ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is diagonal, the singular values of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\sigma }_{1}={\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$): Seeking non-zero solutions of $({A}^{T}\\,A-5I)v=0,$ we obtain the unit eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U,$ $Σ,$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99270a04-7542-5545-92a3-cf13464ef8bc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Columns of the First Matrix Given Part of an SVD With a Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ced37fa-9952-53a4-84c9-51b7b86e1c11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165591} {:answer/id #uuid "9f41e292-7ae3-50a6-bfa2-92a2098012c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165592} {:answer/id #uuid "af15d59e-0f74-52f0-b5dc-f3924fff1b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165593} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a83ffa-d338-56f4-9e86-572177bb09a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165594} {:answer/id #uuid "02927305-e648-5736-9f0e-d96654dde0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165595}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c27122e1-a8f1-573a-9316-534e0793ad74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165590} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8743bfaf-2769-54ae-a264-86b8504ad546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395081} {:answer/id #uuid "f51d771b-a16a-52b0-9a9c-0bdd9295baab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395082} {:answer/id #uuid "7da7d279-0192-57c9-a120-edf1b3f008c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395083} {:answer/id #uuid "795b09e9-e6c6-5744-8a17-7836bc583588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395084} {:answer/id #uuid "32f1cc2f-8169-599b-ba00-8d5ad043bc2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f51d771b-a16a-52b0-9a9c-0bdd9295baab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395082} :answer-field/id #uuid "b63c485f-1fdc-5f96-8275-f5fdc78f32ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5df0e730-65fe-51be-be3c-4af036828478" :question/math-academy-id "q-95731" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${u}_{i}$ and ${v}_{i}$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{10}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0dc37d-a22e-572c-933d-f3490ad356de" :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix With a Zero Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5f1cb32-9a61-59b0-b216-f66534ffd075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$ and $V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404852} {:answer/id #uuid "0bc0298a-ae5a-5828-9130-e084e0abf53d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404853} {:answer/id #uuid "4379fcf7-9c33-5680-a13d-1aaf3147a432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$ and $V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404854} {:answer/id #uuid "c86b61ac-0ec5-5ffe-a535-a8440bb701e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$ and $V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404855} {:answer/id #uuid "67d57ccc-33c5-526d-ac1f-06d33d9d5262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$ and $V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c86b61ac-0ec5-5ffe-a535-a8440bb701e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$ and $V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404855} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3303f09-b1cf-58c2-80d3-c7c5d852de3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c0349f24-f5b9-5625-bd79-9fb74dd2ec96" :question/math-academy-id "q-95981" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that from the matrix $Σ,$ we can deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{10}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$ without solving the characteristic equation.\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=10,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-10I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-0I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A{A}^{T}$): Since the first singular value is not zero, and we know the corresponding singular right vector, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$ \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30b55397-ef25-5e49-bee0-eb619e97b6ad" :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2161ac8-52f4-5d9a-92a3-77afc2763246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]$ and $V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404900} {:answer/id #uuid "c97702d6-0e64-50be-a6e7-cda40c348c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404901} {:answer/id #uuid "952a8fe3-c3ee-5d50-b10a-989312d24249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand $V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404902} {:answer/id #uuid "6ead5f74-265d-5b4c-8a42-a521a55d3bb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand $V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404903} {:answer/id #uuid "46a630d5-42d3-51d9-a41c-4fd7bac52dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand $V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2161ac8-52f4-5d9a-92a3-77afc2763246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]$ and $V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404900} :answer-field/id #uuid "63e8e771-446d-5b36-92ae-de4eeb38d456" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e0475ce-10fb-5b95-b690-55866d1310e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-96411" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{2} & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that since ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is diagonal, the singular values of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\sigma }_{1}={\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$): Seeking non-zero solutions of $({A}^{T}\\,A-3I)v=0,$ we obtain the unit eigenvectors  \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U,$ $Σ,$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}]}
